SSAP legacy containment / historical codec
==========================================

Run: python3 tools/testing/selftests/sparklink/run_ssap_containment.py

The runner compiles the test-only historical ssap/legacy_staging.rs, appending
private child tests, and the actual remote ioctl dispatcher body. KVec/allocation
and the transport TCID constant are explicit dependency fixtures. Nine tests
cover all opcodes and input lengths including 65535/65536, repeated full frames,
no allocation/mutation/ACK on unsupported ingress, no method echo, no unowned
notifications, atomic property replacement after OOM, and local value/permission
boundaries, all reserved operation bits and rejected property registration.
The whole kernel build separately checks production wiring.

legacy_codec.rs preserves the previous offset-format codec only as test material.
Its section references are inherited history, not standard conformance claims.
The offset fields / short-payload completion heuristic do not implement the
message-control fields in T/XS 20001-2025 tables 72, 75 and 76. The unsafe
transaction accumulator is deleted, not copied into a test backend. The original
implementation and failure findings remain in Git history at 75b77fc19613.

No production Engine, remote request builder, automatic notification drain or
method echo uses this codec. Remote ioctls, notifications, indications and the
remaining ingress stub return EOPNOTSUPP; inbound legacy channels are dropped
without parsing, consuming/granting credit or ACKing. Native WS73 typed discovery
and its USB/HCC/BSLE path are unchanged. Local service staging is not publication.

This is containment, not complete R04/R07/R08 remediation. The final userspace
Engine needs standard codecs/fragment flags, explicit peer/generation ownership,
request correlation/deadlines/cancellation, permission/error conformance,
transactional queue/credit submission, memory budgets and real peer tests. Native
PDU sockets, negotiated reliability and indication ACK semantics are still open.

The staging/session model is no longer compiled by the production kernel.
It preserves permissions/OOM/failure history and must not become a new wire Engine.
